                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "TL:DR,\nA good game, but a hard sell to most people at the same time. It has a bit of a Reputation® and it is mostly deserved. I think anyone who likes the genre should give it a try, but also be prepared to throw in the towel halfway.\nOverall a tough metroidvania with some great level/world design, really nice attention to detail and a commitment to riddles and puzzles, but somewhat brought down by frustrating difficulty in parts and some puzzles that are absolutely ridiculous (and kinda poison the well for the rest which are quite devilish but cool).\n\n\n\nThere's some decent non-spoilery guides out there, I recommend using them when you inevitably get stuck.\nI think it's worth experiencing (at least the first half) because there's no metroidvania quite as committed to its world, and making progress feels immensely rewarding and really makes you feel like a true ninjana jones (until you need the guides).\n\n---\n\nMore than half of my playtime was absolutely great, even while trying to solve the puzzles (my save file says 32 hours, so you can see how long I spent trying to work things out. Pens and paper were involved). I made it about 2/3 of the way without any guides, but then like most people who play this, I hit a wall where I was completely stuck. Nowhere to go, nothing worked anywhere and the solution was... not impossible, but highly unlikely.\n\nThere's 2 puzzles I would consider absolutely broken, but even without those the world is so large that the further you go, the harder it gets to progress - many of these riddles are tricky on their own, but made even harder by having so many other puzzle pieces to juggle (while the game is trying to kill you), and it's very easy to dismiss the actual solutions because it's so hard to narrow down the scope of what you're trying to accomplish.\nOn top of that, there's a few questionable decisions and inconsistencies that can make things that aren't even puzzles harder than the puzzles (I spent about 4 hours stuck because a certain background element turned out to be a ladder).\n\nOn the gameplay side, it's pretty hard in general, but in the old school way where a lot of the difficulty comes from the limited control style: the jump is the opposite of nimble, and every challenge is designed around how you overcome that (you will be knocked back down out of so many climbs, you have no idea). The metroidvania equivalent of tank controls almost.\nAlso 90% of enemy types are variations of Goddamned Bats.\nIt mostly works if you're into the genre, but some bits are definitely rage-inducing (especially because they're clearly on purpose). I'm too old and broke to fling controllers, but by golly I was tempted at times.",
